You're seeing this because Invert Call Tree changes how weights are computed. With the tree inverted, every sample is attributed to its deepest known frame, and each callee shows its own weight relative to the total. Self-time that lives in an outer frame still shows up there, so the inverted weights don't sum to 100%.

Quick mental model:

- Normal view: each frame shows weight = samples spent in this frame AND its descendants. That naturally nests, so the top frame = 100%.
- Inverted view: each function shows weight = samples where it was the leaf/deepest frame. A sample that spent time in `get_longest_stab` calling `convex_intersect` and then returning is counted under `convex_intersect` (the leaf), but if another path spent time directly in `get_longest_stab` without descending into a profiled function, that time stays attached to the outer frame. The two sets overlap, so they don't add to 100%.

So "convex_intersect at 22%" means about 22% of all samples bottomed out there. The remainder (the 78%) is split between other leaf functions and unattributed time (inlined code, uninstrumented frames, kernel/third-party code Instruments couldn't fully resolve).

The two signatures you're seeing are exactly the bottleneck:

1. `orourke_cgal::convex_intersect` is the Sutherland–Hodgman / O'Rourke-style convex polygon intersection you wrote in `simplify_geometry.h`. It's called once per live candidate per point (`Pn × n`).
2. `i_polygon::Vertex_data::replacement_event` is CGAL's sweep-line kernel inside `Polygon_2` intersection. It only runs when a polygon is non-convex; in your case it's the convex-hull step on `get_conv_from_grid`.

So the optimization targets are clear:

- Shrink `Pn` by tightening the grid or pruning dead candidates earlier.
- Avoid recomputing convex hull on every input point (the hull rotates slowly; cache and reuse).
- Skip `convex_intersect` for already-dead candidates (the early `dead[i]` guard helps, but consider batch-pruning).
- Keep `convex_intersect` on `std::vector<Point>&` directly instead of going through `Polygon_2` to dodge the sweep-line overhead entirely.

Concise plan:

1. Build the "hottest candidate" list from Instruments (Invert Call Tree, Hide System Libraries on).
2. Add a single instrumentation counter block in `get_longest_stab` that prints, per iteration: live candidates, dead candidates, calls to `segment_within_frechet`, total stream points scanned, and the running size of `S[i]`.
3. Optimize in this order: cache the hull → reduce `Pn` (tighter grid or coarser angular spacing) → cut `segment_within_frechet` calls (skip endpoint candidates whose bounding box is already outside the per-iteration window).
4. Re-profile. Stop when no single callee exceeds ~15% under Invert Call Tree.

Sources for the symbols and call sites I cited:

- The candidate-update loop that calls `convex_intersect` via `intersect(F_work[i], Gi, new_S[i])`:

```486:511:simplify.cpp
    while (cur < int(stream.size())) {
        const Point& pi  = stream[cur];
        Gi = get_conv_from_grid(pi, EPSILON, DELTA);
        std::fill(new_dead.begin(), new_dead.end(), 0);
        for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
            if (dead[i]) continue;
            find_F(P[i], S[i], F_work[i]);
            if (!intersect(F_work[i], Gi, new_S[i])) {
                new_dead[i] = 1;
            }
        }
```

- The convex-hull call on every input point:

```209:214:simplify.cpp
inline std::vector<Point> get_conv_from_grid(const Point& p, double EPSILON, double DELTA) {
    std::vector<Point> points = get_points_from_grid(p, EPSILON, DELTA);
    std::vector<Point> conv;
    CGAL::convex_hull_2(points.begin(), points.end(), std::back_inserter(conv));
    return conv;
}
```

Let me know what the convex-hull costs look like and whether `segment_within_frechet` shows up under Invert Call Tree — those two numbers decide the next step.

