`F_work[i]` means “the working buffer that holds the current \(F_i\) polygon for candidate start point `P[i]`.”

In this algorithm there is not just one \(F\). There is one state per grid candidate `P[i]`:

```474:483:/Users/sinchunyeung/Simplification-of-Trajectory-Streams/simplify.cpp
    int p0cur = cur;
    std::vector<Point> P = get_points_from_grid(p0, EPSILON, DELTA);
    std::array<Point, 2> buffer = {p0, p0};
    const int Pn = (int)P.size();
    std::vector<std::vector<Point>> S(Pn, std::vector<Point>{p0});
    int dead_cnt = 0;
    std::vector<int> dead(Pn);
    std::vector<std::vector<Point>> new_S(Pn);
    std::vector<char> new_dead(Pn, 0);
    std::vector<std::vector<Point>> F_work(Pn);
```

`P` is the list of possible snapped/grid start points near `p0`. For each `P[i]`, the code keeps:

- `S[i]`: the currently feasible endpoint region for that candidate.
- `F_work[i]`: a temporary \(F_i\) region computed from `P[i]` and `S[i]`.
- `new_S[i]`: the next feasible endpoint region after intersecting with the current point's grid cell polygon `Gi`.
- `dead[i]`: whether that candidate can no longer work.

The key loop is:

```491:496:/Users/sinchunyeung/Simplification-of-Trajectory-Streams/simplify.cpp
        for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
            if (dead[i]) continue;
            find_F(P[i], S[i], F_work[i]);
            if (!intersect(F_work[i], Gi, new_S[i])) {
                new_dead[i] = 1;
            }
```

So mathematically, for candidate \(i\), it does:

\[
F_i = \operatorname{find\_F}(P_i, S_i)
\]

then:

\[
S_i' = F_i \cap G_i
\]

The code name `F_work` is used because the vector is **scratch/work storage**. `find_F(...)` clears and rewrites the output vector every iteration:

```263:265:/Users/sinchunyeung/Simplification-of-Trajectory-Streams/simplify.cpp
inline void find_F(const Point& p, const std::vector<Point>& S,
                   std::vector<Point>& F) {
    F.clear();
```

Why not just `F`?

Because plain `F` would suggest one global/current polygon, but this loop has many candidate-specific \(F_i\) polygons. A single `std::vector<Point> F;` would work for the non-GUI version if `F` is only needed inside the loop, but `F_work(Pn)` makes the indexing parallel to `S`, `new_S`, and `dead`, and the GUI version also caches/display-checks these per candidate. So `F_work[i]` is more explicit: “temporary \(F\) for candidate `i`,” not “the one \(F\).”

