They represent two different states of a grid-point candidate \(P[i]\):

- `dead[i]`: candidate was already impossible **before** processing the current input point.
- `new_dead[i]`: candidate became impossible **while processing** the current input point.

The sequence in `get_longest_stab()` is:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
    if (dead[i]) continue;

    find_F(P[i], S[i], F[i]);
    if (!intersect(F[i], Gi, new_S[i])) {
        new_dead[i] = 1;
    }
}
```

For each candidate that is still alive from prior vertices:

1. Compute its propagated feasible region `F[i]`.
2. Intersect it with the new grid-convex region `Gi`.
3. If that intersection is empty, it cannot continue through the current input point, so mark it in `new_dead`.

Then, before committing the iteration, the code checks whether **any candidate survived this new point**:

```cpp
if (dead[i] || new_dead[i]) continue;
if (!new_S[i].empty()) {
    buffer[0] = P[i];
    buffer[1] = new_S[i].front();
    has_candidate = true;
}
```

Only if at least one candidate survived do we make the deaths permanent:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
    if (new_dead[i]) {
        dead[i] = true;
        dead_cnt++;
    }
}
```

So `new_dead` is a temporary “transaction result” for the current vertex. It lets the algorithm:

- evaluate all candidates against the same `pi`,
- determine whether the current vertex can be included at all,
- update `dead` and `S` only after that iteration succeeds.

If no candidate survives, the loop stops. The valid simplification segment ends at the **previous** input point, using the last valid `buffer`. In that situation, committing `new_dead` would not be useful because the current point is rejected.

Could this be implemented with one array? Yes, technically: update `dead[i]` immediately and carefully preserve the prior valid `buffer`. But two arrays make the algorithm’s phases explicit:

- `dead` = state after the last accepted input point.
- `new_dead` = proposed state after attempting the next input point.

That separation is especially helpful because `new_S[i]` is also a proposed next-state region, while `S[i]` remains the last accepted-state region until the whole iteration succeeds.

Yes — in this implementation, you can use just `dead`.

`new_dead` is a two-phase / transactional style:

- compute proposed deaths into `new_dead`
- check whether any candidate survives
- commit those deaths into `dead`

But there is no later operation in the iteration that needs to distinguish:

- “was dead before this input point,” from
- “died because of this input point.”

Once `intersect(F[i], Gi, new_S[i])` is empty, that candidate can be marked dead immediately.

The important ordering is only:

1. finish computing every active candidate’s `new_S[i]`;
2. mark failed candidates dead;
3. select an alive candidate for `buffer`;
4. if none survived, stop without advancing `cur`;
5. swap `S[i]` with `new_S[i]` only for surviving candidates.

That can be written with one array:

```cpp
std::fill(new_dead.begin(), new_dead.end(), 0);

for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
    if (dead[i]) continue;

    find_F(P[i], S[i], F[i]);
    if (!intersect(F[i], Gi, new_S[i])) {
        dead[i] = true;
        ++dead_cnt;
    }
}

bool has_candidate = false;
for (int i = Pn - 1; i >= 0 && !has_candidate; --i) {
    if (dead[i] || new_S[i].empty()) continue;

    buffer[0] = P[i];
    buffer[1] = new_S[i].front();
    has_candidate = true;
}

if (!has_candidate) break;

for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
    if (!dead[i]) {
        S[i].swap(new_S[i]);
    }
}
```

Then remove:

- `std::vector<char> new_dead(Pn, 0);`
- its reset with `std::fill`
- the separate loop that copies `new_dead[i]` into `dead[i]`.

The only caveat is the GUI version: it currently uses `new_dead` while deciding which regions to draw. That condition:

```cpp
if (dead[i] || new_dead[i]) continue;
```

simply becomes:

```cpp
if (dead[i]) continue;
```

So yes, `new_dead` does not provide an essential correctness property here; it mainly makes the “proposed next state, then commit” structure mirror `new_S`.

